    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Seagulls have an eye for good food. New research suggests they prefer food that humans have already touched. This may explain why the species has done so well in "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b1",
            "number": 1
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Today, many of these birds live closely alongside humans."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“UK "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "herring gull",
            "zh": "银鸥"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " numbers are decreasing, but urban populations have "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b2",
            "number": 2
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ",” says "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "zoologist",
            "zh": "动物学家"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " Madeleine Goumas from the University of Exeter. “They’re a "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b3",
            "number": 3
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " sight in many towns. However, little is known about urban gull behaviour.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Earlier research by Goumas and her colleagues found that gulls might be able to read human "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "cues",
            "ref": "v-cue"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". In one experiment, they could be driven away from a picnic table "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b4",
            "number": 4
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " by eye contact with the humans at the table. But there wasn’t enough "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "evidence",
            "ref": "v-evidence"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to prove this "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b5",
            "number": 5
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " in the wild birds."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Now, researchers have found more proof that gulls looking for food in cities take cues from their human neighbours. In an experiment, scientists hid food under two "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "overturned buckets",
            "zh": "倒扣的桶"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " near dozens of gulls. The buckets were then "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b6",
            "number": 6
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " at the same time to reveal a "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "wrapped",
            "ref": "v-wrapped"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " pancake underneath. One of the scientists then picked up one of the pancakes and pretended to "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b7",
            "number": 7
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " it. About 20 seconds later, she put it back on the ground."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Of the 38 gulls tested, 24 "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "pecked",
            "zh": "啄"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " at one of the pancakes. Nearly 80 percent chose the one that had been picked up by the human. “This shows that human touch attracts the attention of gulls. Handled food is more "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b8",
            "number": 8
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to them than food left untouched,” the authors wrote."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "To "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "determine",
            "ref": "v-determine"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " whether humans alone are enough to attract gulls to an item, the team "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b9",
            "number": 9
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " the experiment. This time, they used two "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "sponges",
            "zh": "海绵"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " instead of pancakes. The gulls were not so easily "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b10",
            "number": 10
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". More of the birds approached the sponges without pecking at them."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
